  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nClouds have been increasing and will continue to increase this\nafternoon into tonight as our next system approaches. We did end\nup breaking the 60 degree mark again today across much of\neastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin. Looking ahead we don't\nlook likely to break 60 again until maybe a week from now when\nthe NBM 75th percentile has highs in the 60s. Overall this next \nsystem looks to follow a clipper path and typical impacts. This\nwill be rain showers generally along and north of I-94 tonight \ninto tomorrow morning based on where the forcing is expected to \nsetup for this clipper track. Behind this we will see a cold frontal\npassage. This will bring temperatures down from the much above \nnormal start to the week. Also across western Wisconsin we will \nsee some additional precipitation chances later in the day \ntomorrow along this front, as this area will see better low \nlevel saturation. There could even, in areas like Rusk County, \nbe some snow flakes mixing in at times. With ground and air \ntemperatures near to only slightly below freezing nothing is \nexpected to stick or accumulate. Highs will vary tomorrow from \nthe 40s across central Minnesota to the lower 50s farther south \nbased on the timing of peak diurnal heating vs frontal passage. \nAs the front will be moving from north to south across the \nregion.\n\nThe airmass behind this frontal passage will bring\nhighs down into the mid 30s to lower 40s and lows into the 20s\nfor Thursday and Friday. This also comes in with persistent \nhigh pressure which will keep precipitation chance low to non- \nexistent. The post frontal CAA will start to fall off on \nSaturday allowing for some warm up this weekend. Highs in the \n40s appear most likely. We turn to some warmer air advecting in \nto start the next week which should bring back highs in the 50s.\nWith the ridge persisting, precipitation chances remain low.",
